{
  "configuration_id": "config_011",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_007 (multimodal, singleprofile, scvi_n200+resnet50, concat) achieved 0.5362 acc / 0.5158 F1, modestly outperforming config_033 (rna_only, singleprofile, scvi_n200) at 0.5203 acc / 0.4982 F1. This suggests a small but consistent benefit from the image modality, but we have no evidence yet about whether multiprofile aggregation helps. Uncertainty: The profile setting (single vs. multi) is a fundamental design axis we have not explored; all completed experiments used singleprofile. Question: Does moving from singleprofile to multiprofile with simple avgpool aggregation improve validation accuracy/F1 while keeping the same encoders (scvi_n200, resnet50) and fusion (concat) as our best config? Why this is the best next step: config_011 is the minimal, single-axis extension of config_007 (only profile_setting changes, with the necessary addition of avgpool aggregation that multiprofile requires). It isolates the effect of profile diversity and aggregation without simultaneously changing encoders or fusion, making the result interpretable. If it improves, it validates multiprofile as a productive direction; if not, it confirms singleprofile sufficiency and redirects budget toward encoder or fusion variations."
}

